UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 23, Lesson 1: Mixtures and separating them', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism, solubility).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich property helps separate: pebbles from sand; paperclips from rice?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSize (sieve); magnetism.\n\nWATCH FOR\nStirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign separation plans for four mixtures. Name the property each plan uses. Try two safe separations and record results.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism, solubility).","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design separation plans for four mixtures. Name the property each plan uses. Try two safe separations and record results. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about mixture linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself.. Correction: Size (sieve); magnetism. with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses mixture. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about mixture, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about mixture. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for mixture. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for mixture. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Size (sieve); magnetism","Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Which property helps separate: pebbles from sand; paperclips from rice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for mixture; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism","Size (sieve); magnetism","Careful use of separate and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with mixture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named separate and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","Name separate and explain why it matters only, without linking it to mixture","Apply mixture to new material and name the separate and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies mixture with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself"],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, a glass marble, a fallen dry leaf. Which claim about mixture is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A label copied from the title with no evidence","Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about mixture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about mixture with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Size (sieve); magnetism"],"question":"Using this science idea — Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about mixture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any exciting story about the object must be true","Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about mixture should change with it"],"question":"Which prediction about mixture can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about mixture. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses mixture. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about mixture linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for mixture. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about mixture, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself.. Correction: Size (sieve); magnetism. with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w23-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise mixture.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Material World","success_criteria":["I can use mixture with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify separate and explain why it matters.","I can practise combine independently on a new prompt.","I can notice property needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism, solubility).","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixture with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTrail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different methods (e.g. evaporate later).\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich property helps separate: pebbles from sand; paperclips from rice?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSize (sieve); magnetism.","explain":"Mixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism, solubility).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Mixtures and separation.","caption":"Stirring alone ≠ separate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Mixture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">combined materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Separate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">use properties</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Trail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Trail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixture with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTrail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different methods (e.g. evaporate later). Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Property-based plans.","caption":"Four plans; two tries.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Magnet / sieve</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">property tools</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stir to separate</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixture with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself. Here is a more productive model: Trail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different methods (e.g. evaporate later).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSize (sieve); magnetism.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Stirring harder does not separate a mixture by itself. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixture with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMixtures combine materials that can often be separated again using differences in properties (size, magnetism, solubility). Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 23, Lesson 1: Mixtures and separating them","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Mixtures and changes","week":23,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nTrail mix: pick out by size/type. Sand + iron filings: magnet. Sand + water: settle/filter. Salt + water needs different methods (e.g. evaporate later).\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Mixtures and separation.","caption":"Stirring alone ≠ separate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Mixture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">combined materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Separate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">use properties</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Property-based plans.","caption":"Four plans; two tries.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\